        "# EXERCISE 2 — Feature Selection\n",
        "\n",
        "You work for a new mobile phone company. Before releasing a phone, the team wants to know its **price range** (low, medium, high or very high cost). They collected the specs of 2,000 phones and their price ranges.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Goal:** Predict the price range of a phone from its specs, and find out **which specs really matter**.\n",
        "\n",
        "The dataset has **20 features**: battery, RAM, cameras, screen size, Bluetooth, WiFi, and more. Some of them probably say nothing about the price. **Feature selection** keeps only the relevant features. This can prevent the model from learning from **noise**, **improve** (or keep) the accuracy, and **reduce** training time.\n",
        "\n",
        "You will use the **three feature selection methods** from the lecture:\n",
        "\n",
        "| Method | When does it choose the features? | How it works | What we use |\n",
        "|---|---|---|---|\n",
        "| **Filter** | **Before** training (a pre-processing step) | Gives each feature a **score** based on how related it is to the label. Does **not** use a learning algorithm. | `SelectKBest` + ANOVA F-test (`f_classif`) |\n",
        "| **Wrapper** | **Around** the model | **Trains the model again and again** on different subsets of features, and keeps the subset that gives the best result. | `RFECV` (Recursive Feature Elimination + Cross-Validation) |\n",
        "| **Embedded** | **During** training | The model has its **own built-in way** to measure how important each feature is. | `RandomForestClassifier` + `SelectFromModel` |\n",
        "\n",
        "## Your Goal\n",
        "\n",
        "At the end of this exercise, your results should answer **three questions**:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. **Which phone specs really decide the price?** Each method gives you a list of selected features. `RAM` should be at the top in every method, while specs like `Bluetooth` and `WiFi` should be dropped.\n",
        "2. **How many features do we really need?** Only about **4 to 7 of the 20** features.\n",
        "3. **Does using fewer features make the model worse?** **No.** Your models with selected features should reach the **same or higher accuracy** than the baseline that uses all 20 features (about **0.96**):\n",
        "\n",
        "| Model | Number of features | Target test accuracy |\n",
        "|---|---|---|\n",
        "| Baseline (all features, given) | 20 | about **0.96** |\n",
        "| Part 1 — Filter | 4 | about **0.97** |\n",
        "| Part 2 — Wrapper | about 7 | about **0.99** |\n",
        "| Part 3 — Embedded | 4 | about **0.97** |\n",
        "\n",
        "**Final result:** the comparison table and chart in Section 13 should show that a model using **only a few relevant features** predicts the price range **as well as or better than** a model using all 20 features.\n",
        "\n",
        "*Tip: the Demo notebook (`Demo_Feature_Selection.ipynb`) follows the same steps with a different dataset.*\n",
        "\n",
        "Dataset: https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/iabhishekofficial/mobile-price-classification\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## Objectives\n",
        "\n",
        "By the end of this exercise, you should be able to:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. Explain the difference between the **filter**, **wrapper** and **embedded** methods.\n",
        "2. Select features with `SelectKBest`, `RFECV` and `SelectFromModel` using scikit-learn.\n",
        "3. Train a model on the selected features only and measure its **accuracy**.\n",
        "4. **Visualize** the result of each method.\n",
        "5. Compare the three methods with a model that uses all features.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Expected Outcomes\n",
        "\n",
        "When you finish, your notebook should have:\n",
        "\n",
        "| Section | Expected outcome |\n",
        "|---|---|\n",
        "| 3 *(given)* | Baseline accuracy with **all 20 features**: about **0.96** |\n",
        "| 4–6 | **Filter:** 4 selected features, accuracy about **0.97**, and a bar chart of F-scores |\n",
        "| 7–9 | **Wrapper:** best number about **7** features, accuracy about **0.99**, and a line chart of accuracy vs number of features |\n",
        "| 10–12 | **Embedded:** 4 selected features, accuracy about **0.97**, and a bar chart of importances with a red threshold line |\n",
        "| 13 | A comparison table and bar chart of all methods |\n",
        "\n",
        "`RAM` should be the **most important feature in every method**."
      ]
